# A thread nobody waits for aborts when `main` returns, saying how many, and
# names the line its `spawn` stands on (spec § 13; panel 251's R2; feature
# 2647).
#
# The thread waits on a mailbox nobody sends to, so it is still running when
# `main` returns, on every platform and at every level. Until 2026-11-25 it
# answered at once, and whether it had ended by then was a race, which the
# CI's Windows leg lost at `-O2` (run of fc19cb341: `1 of them still running`
# where the case said 0). Its end cannot be made certain instead: the runtime
# marks a thread finished after its function has returned
# (`runtime/parts/spawn.c`, `hero_spawn_leave`), which nothing a program waits
# on reaches but the `wait` this case leaves out.

function idle(inbox: (mailbox i64)) -> i64
    return inbox.receive(within_ms: -1).default(0)

function main()
    inbox: (mailbox i64) = mailbox()
    t = spawn(idle, inbox).must()
    _ = t
    print("forgot")
